  "live_records": {
    "q-36518": {
      "dom_id": "step-q36518",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=x+4y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=3x-y$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=0$\n\nIII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{y}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$",
      "worked_solution": "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+4y)=4 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x-y)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x)=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(0)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{y}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
