    "q-80761": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80761",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. ${f}_{x}(x,y)={x}^{2}-2y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={y}^{2}-2x$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{x}}+3{x}^{2}y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}+{x}^{3}$\n\nIII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{x+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1)$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{\\sqrt{y}}$",
      "worked_solution": "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2y)=-2 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-2x)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{x}}+3{x}^{2}y)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{yx}}+3{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}+{x}^{3})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{xy}}+3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1))=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{\\sqrt{y}})=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II and III'.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
