    "q-133372": {
      "dom_id": "step-q133372",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Given that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ evaluate ${f}_{yxy}(1,2)$ if ${f}_{yy}(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y).$",
      "worked_solution": "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yxy}(x,y) & ={f}_{yyx}(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{yy}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y)) \\\\  & ={y}^{3}(2x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y)+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y)\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2x{y}^{3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-y)+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{xyx}(1,2)=2\\cdot 1\\cdot {2}^{3}\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 1-2)+1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 1-2))=16.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
